# Node.js + Nginx Reverse Proxy (Docker)

## Overview

This project demonstrates how to deploy a **Node.js (Express)** application behind **Nginx** using Docker.

The setup mimics a production environment where:

* Nginx acts as a reverse proxy.
* Node.js serves the application.
* Docker packages everything into a container.

---

## Architecture

```text
Browser
    │
localhost:8080
    │
Docker Container
    │
Nginx (Port 80)
    │
Node.js Express (Port 3000)
```

---

## Project Structure

```text
.
├── app.js
├── package.json
├── Dockerfile
├── nginx.conf
└── README.md
```

---

## Prerequisites

* Docker Desktop
* Node.js (for local development)
* Git

---

## Run Locally

Install dependencies:

```bash
npm install
```

Start the application:

```bash
node app.js
```

Open:

```
http://localhost:3000
```

---

## Build Docker Image

```bash
docker build -t node-nginx .
```

---

## Run Docker Container

```bash
docker run -d \
  --name node-nginx \
  -p 8080:80 \
  node-nginx
```

---

## Access the Application

Open your browser:

```
http://localhost:8080
```

Request flow:

```text
Browser
      │
localhost:8080
      │
Docker Port Mapping
      │
Nginx
      │
Node.js
```

---

## Verify Container

List running containers:

```bash
docker ps
```

View logs:

```bash
docker logs node-nginx
```

Open a shell inside the container:

```bash
docker exec -it node-nginx sh
```

---

## Stop and Remove Container

Stop:

```bash
docker stop node-nginx
```

Remove:

```bash
docker rm node-nginx
```

Remove image:

```bash
docker rmi node-nginx
```

---

## Nginx Reverse Proxy

Example configuration:

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;

        proxy_http_version 1.1;

        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
    }
}
```

---

